-- title:
--   All noiseless observations from the unit ball lie in a set of reference mass at most $192^d$
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space of dimension $d$, with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\gamma_E$ be the standard Gaussian measure on $E$ (its coordinates in any orthonormal basis are independent standard normal variables). For $m\ge0$ write $\lambda_m$ for Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}^m$, and let $R_m=\gamma_E^{\otimes m}\otimes\lambda_m$ be the reference measure on $E^m\times\mathbb{R}^m$ (Gaussian rows $A=(A_1,\dots,A_m)$ and a label vector in $\mathbb{R}^m$). Assume $m\le d$. Then there is a measurable set $D\subseteq E^m\times\mathbb{R}^m$ such that
--
--   1. $D$ contains every noiseless observation from the closed unit ball: for every $A\in E^m$ and every $t\in E$ with $\|t\|\le1$,
--   $$\bigl(A,(\langle t,A_1\rangle,\dots,\langle t,A_m\rangle)\bigr)\in D;$$
--   2. $R_m(D)\le192^{d}$.
--
--   So all possible noiseless observations generated by unknown vectors of norm at most $1$ fit into one fixed window whose reference mass is exponential in the dimension $d$, uniformly in the number of rows $m\le d$. This lets density and Hölder-type bounds relative to $R_m$ be applied on a set of controlled reference mass.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $R_m$ is `matrixLabelReference m`, and $d$ is `Module.finrank ℝ E`; the bound is stated in $[0,\infty]$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Probability/Subsphere/Regression/Projection.lean#L414-L446

